# One recorded suite map, and the run list `le functional select` answers from it.
#
# The functional stage asks two questions before it builds anything: which
# packages this change set reaches, and which packages each suite reached when
# it last ran. This test drives the second half for real. The fixture writes a
# map into a scratch checkout, publishes a change set through
# ZE_VERIFY_SCOPE_PACKAGES, and pins what a gating run would then start.
#
# It proves the SELECTION and nothing about the RECORDING. A map written by a
# fixture is not an instrumented run's observation, and treating it as one would
# be a scenario that passes against a map nobody ever produced. What a suite
# reached is asserted by the reduction's own tests
# (`internal/le/functional/reach_test.go`).
#
# The three widening branches are what carry the safety argument, so each one is
# a scenario of its own. A package the map never recorded, a package a commit
# touched since the recording, and no map at all each run every suite, and each
# one says which condition it met. A map that narrowed on a reason it could not
# check is the failure this test exists to catch, and reading `narrowed` alone
# would not catch it: `unknown-package-runs-every-suite-and-is-named` also
# requires the answer to name the package.
#
# `operator-skip-outranks-the-map` is the fourth. ZE_SKIP_SUITES is read before
# the map, so a recorded set can subtract a suite and can never add back one the
# operator removed.

option=timeout:value=180s




cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze-test fixture runner/verify-scope-suite-map
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=map-narrows-to-the-suites-that-reached-the-package
expect=stdout:contains=unknown-package-runs-every-suite-and-is-named
expect=stdout:contains=operator-skip-outranks-the-map
expect=stdout:contains=a-commit-since-the-recording-makes-the-package-unknown
expect=stdout:contains=an-absent-map-runs-every-suite
